Cross-check of the optional files: the copies at
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/LoupVaillant/Monocypher/4.0.2/src/optional/monocypher-ed25519.c
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/LoupVaillant/Monocypher/4.0.2/src/optional/monocypher-ed25519.h
differ from the tarball's only in the first line ("__git__" instead of
"4.0.2"), the same release-script substitution as monocypher.c. So all four
files are the 4.0.2 release.

Why monocypher-ed25519 (DESIGN.md §1.2 D4, §2, A1): Kome signs with RFC 8032
Ed25519, SHA-512 inside the signature only. Core monocypher's crypto_eddsa_*
is EdDSA with BLAKE2b-512, which is not RFC 8032 and is no longer used for
signatures. Kome.Crypto binds crypto_ed25519_key_pair, crypto_ed25519_sign
and crypto_ed25519_check; BLAKE2b, X25519, XChaCha20-Poly1305 and crypto_wipe
still come from monocypher.c.

Note on strictness: crypto_ed25519_check refuses S >= L but, by design,
accepts non-canonical encodings of R and A (y >= p, or x = 0 with the sign
bit set). Wycheproof ed25519_test.json tcId 151 is such a signature and
crypto_ed25519_check accepts it. Kome.Crypto.Verify therefore checks the
RFC 8032 §5.1.3 encodings of R and A itself before calling
crypto_ed25519_check (see kome-crypto.ads, Is_Canonical_Point).
